    "q-143740": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143740",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14274884/assets/eb5f10ce887ba9d167b2753618064fcdbf8d1f42371d733aacd56a8d8cb5e667.png)\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$",
      "worked_solution": "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the point $(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$ on our diagram. Let's also consider the region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14274884/assets/dd7024bcd41ce8312f484763e0988ebcceb54e7154cd8ee3c209991bce5727c1.png)\n\nUsing the definition of the joint CDF, we have\n\n$$\nF(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2})=P(X<-\\frac{3}{2},Y<\\frac{1}{2})={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3/2}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nHowever, $f(u,v)$ is zero everywhere inside the region $Δ.$ Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3/2}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
