    "q-143705": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143705",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(3,6)=0.8,\\,F(-2,3)=0.45,\\,F(-2,6)=0.15.\n$$\n\nFind $P(-2\\le X\\le 3,Y\\le 6).$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the probability that the pair $(X,Y)$ will lie inside the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14274884/assets/be1e5d3caaacbde5afb42bd49e36279aabb99d0c64f51e182211ccb55f840ab0.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(3,6)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(-2,6)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14274884/assets/4753950503d735091c95c96b025ed480c26ae775957ad95d9044a416dac4659a.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-2\\le X\\le 3,Y\\le 6) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(3,6)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 3,Y\\le 6)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(-2,6)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le -2,Y\\le 6)}} \\\\  & =F(3,6)-F(-2,6) \\\\  & =0.8-0.15 \\\\  & =0.65.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
